# Step 10 draft angles: ${vault_tag}

Modality: collect (a per-tenure-type catalogue) plus synthesize (patterns). Register: survey.
Citation: `[[note-id|pinpoint]]`, converted at delivery. Catalogue spine: `${catalogue_dir}/arrangements.csv`
(record ids) and `matrix.md`. The structure is fixed by the skill: sections 1 to 8 and Annexes;
section 2 is subdivided by tenure type (formal-law types, then non-formal types, then unclassified),
each subsection opening with a definition line and a count, then the arrangements by sub-basin and
named point.

- **Draft A: breadth by tenure type.** Widest coverage: every FAO type found and every audited
  absence, each with its full set of named arrangements and places. Optimises for completeness of
  the typology and of the place list; brief entries (local name, place, users, mechanism, citation,
  record id).
- **Draft B: depth on contested classifications.** The three to five hardest typing calls argued
  against their counter-readings, still under the tenure-type headings. Typical candidates: plan
  against practice (paper water against wet water); extraction that sits between water law and
  mining law; uses tolerated by the agency (agency control and commonhold, or assumed?); community
  systems moving from assumed to permitted; customary institutions that the state has absorbed; links
  between land tenure and water use.
- **Draft C: place and practitioner.** Within each type, arrangements ordered sub-basin > named point,
  with administrative location and coordinates; foregrounds users, upstream-downstream position,
  seasonality, exclusion, and the fieldwork questions to ask at each named place. Strongest on
  sections 3, 5 and 6 and on the gazetteer annex.
